---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AtmosphereNav from '../../../components/AtmosphereNav.astro';
---

<Layout>
	<Breadcrumb crumbs={[
		{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
		{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' },
		{ label: 'U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator' },
	]} />
	<h1>U.S. Standard Atmosphere Calculator</h1>
	<p>Tabulate atmospheric properties as a function of altitude using the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Standard_Atmosphere" target="_blank">U.S. Standard Atmosphere 1976 model</a>.</p>
	<AtmosphereNav active="notes" />

	<h2>About the U.S. Standard Atmosphere</h2>
	<p>
	The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Standard_Atmosphere" target="_blank">U.S. Standard Atmosphere</a> models atmospheric 
	pressure, temperature, and other properties by altitude from -5 to 1000 km 
	(-16404 to 3280840 ft, -3.11 to 621.37 mi). Formulas are derived
	from <a href="https://www.pdas.com/atmosdownload.html" target="_blank">the 1976 U.S. Standard Atmosphere of 1976</a>, covering the lower atmosphere
	from -5 to 86 km and the upper atmosphere from 86 to 1000 km. This calculator uses the updated values of the Avogadro and gas constants fixed in 
	the 2019 SI revision, rather than the values used in the 1976 U.S. standard atmosphere. In addition, it uses updated gas concentrations from NOAA's
	GML to better represent current atmospheric chemistry, particularly the increase in CO<sub>2</sub>, resulting in a slightly higher molar mass at
	sea level (28.9657 g/mol, rather than 28.9644).
	</p>
	<p>
	The boiling point of water is calculated 
	using <a href="https://www.iapws.org/relguide/IF97-Rev.html" target="_blank">IAPWS-95</a> and is accurate to 0.01°C (0.02°F). At pressures below 
	water's triple point of 611.657 Pa, the boiling point is undefined because liquid water cannot exist.
	</p>
	<h2>Standard sea-level conditions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Pressure: 1 atm (101325 Pa, 1013.25 hPa, 760 mmHg, 14.6959 psi, 29.9213 inHg)</li>
		<li>Temperature: 15°C (59°F, 288.15 K, 518.67°R)</li>
		<li>Lapse rate: -6.5°C/km (0.00357°F/ft) under 11 km, varies above 11 km</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Changes with altitude</h2>
	<p>
	In the troposphere (below 11 km), temperature decreases with altitude at the lapse rate - about 6.5°C/km (0.00357°F/ft) - while pressure decreases
	roughly exponentially, dropping by half about every 5500 m (18000 ft). Above that, the stratosphere (11-51 km) holds steady before warming 
	with altitude; the mesosphere (51-86 km) cools again; and the thermosphere (86-1000 km) warms again. Pressure keeps falling roughly exponentially 
	throughout.
	</p>
	<p>
	Sea-level pressure is reported two ways. Sea-level pressure proper extrapolates a station's observed pressure down using its actual temperature and 
	the standard lapse rate. Altimeter setting instead assumes sea level is always at the standard 15°C, regardless of the real temperature. Since warm air
	expands the column and raises pressure aloft relative to cold air at the same sea-level pressure, altimeter setting usually reads higher than 
	sea-level pressure in summer and lower in winter.
	</p>
	<p>
	Example: at Everest's summit (8849 m, 29032 ft), typical summer conditions 
	are <a href="https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wea.3931" target="_blank">-19°C (-2°F)</a> and <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10066724/" target="_blank">336 hPa (0.332 atm)</a>. 
	Extrapolating -19°C to sea level with the standard lapse rate gives 37.4°C (99.3°F) - far above the standard 15°C - and a sea-level pressure of 980 
	hPa. Altimeter setting instead assumes the standard 15°C at sea level and asks what pressure would produce that same 336 hPa at 8849 m: 1081 hPa.
	</p>
	<p>
	The U.S. Standard Atmosphere models a "typical" mid-latitude profile. At a given altitude, it underestimates pressure in the tropics and in summer, 
	and overestimates it in polar regions and in winter. Above the troposphere, it is most accurate when anchored to standard sea-level conditions: 
	15°C and 1 atm. Due to the break between the lower atmosphere below 86 km and the upper atmosphere above, some properties (such as density) have
	discontinuities at this point. The U.S. Standard Atmosphere is not a weather model, and it does not account for local variations in pressure, 
	temperature, or humidity (it assumes 0% humidity throughout).
	</p>
	<h2>Geometric versus geopotential altitude</h2>
	<p>
	Geometric altitude is the actual height above mean sea level, while geopotential altitude is a "corrected" altitude representing the altitude in a
	uniform gravitational field of 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup> that would require the same work to reach as the actual altitude in the real gravity field. 
	Gravity weakens slightly at higher altitudes, due to the greater distance from earth's center, so at very high altitudes geopotential altitude is 
	significantly less than geometric altitude.
	</p>
</Layout>
